arXiv:2509.20721v1 [cs.LG] 25 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuda Bi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>TReNDS Center, Georgia State University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Georgia Institute of Technology &amp; Emory University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Atlanta, GA, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ybi3@gsu.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vince D. Calhoun
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>TReNDS Center, Georgia State University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Georgia Institute of Technology &amp; Emory University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Atlanta, GA, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">vcalhoun@gsu.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling laws, a defining feature of deep learning, reveal a striking power-law improvement in model performance with increasing dataset and model size. Yet, their mathematical origins, especially the scaling exponent, have remained elusive. In this work, we show that scaling laws can be formally explained as <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">redundancy laws</em>. Using kernel regression, we show that a polynomial tail in the data covariance spectrum yields an excess risk power law with exponent <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> controls the spectral tail and <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math> measures redundancy. This reveals that the learning curve’s slope is not universal but depends on data redundancy, with steeper spectra accelerating returns to scale. We establish the law’s universality across boundedly invertible transformations, multi-modal mixtures, finite-width approximations, and Transformer architectures in both linearized (NTK) and feature-learning regimes. This work delivers the first rigorous mathematical explanation of scaling laws as finite-sample redundancy laws, unifying empirical observations with theoretical foundations.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">A Preprint
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling laws have emerged as one of the most striking and influential empirical discoveries in modern machine learning, shaping both scientific understanding and industrial practice. Across domains ranging from natural language processing (NLP) and computer vision (CV) to multi-modal systems, performance metrics such as perplexity or classification error have been shown to follow remarkably consistent power-law curves as models, data, and compute are scaled <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Henighan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rosenfeld, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghajanyan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For instance, in large language models like GPT-3, perplexity decreases predictably with training tokens, obeying</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(n)\;\propto\;n^{-\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(n)\;\propto\;n^{-\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the dataset size and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the scaling exponent governing the rate of improvement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Similar scaling behavior has been observed in vision models trained on benchmarks such as ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Henighan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and in generative and multimodal architectures including Transformers and diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These predictable curves have become a practical cornerstone: they inform dataset sizing, architecture design, and compute allocation, and have even guided the development of compute-optimal training strategies such as Chinchilla <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their ubiquity, the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mathematical origin</em> of scaling laws remains unresolved. Why do error curves so often collapse to power laws? What determines the exponent <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and why does it differ across domains, tasks, and modalities? Addressing these questions is critical for predicting performance in new regimes and for designing models that scale more efficiently. Current explanations are fragmented: some attribute scaling to statistical properties like Zipfian distributions in language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Henighan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>; others emphasize optimization dynamics such as stochastic gradient descent (SGD) and implicit regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2019</a>)</cite>; while still others propose information-theoretic perspectives based on predictability and complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bialek et al., 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rodríguez-Gálvez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. More recent works attempt to systematize these views <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bahri et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghajanyan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sengupta et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but no framework yet delivers a closed-form expression for <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or unifies scaling behavior across architectures, modalities, and data distributions. This gap between empirical regularity and theoretical understanding is now one of the central open problems of the deep learning era <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caballero et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Michaud et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we argue that scaling laws are fundamentally <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">redundancy laws</em>. Specifically, we show that the scaling exponent is governed by the redundancy of the data representation, captured by the polynomial tail of its covariance spectrum. Using classical statistical learning theory for kernel regression, we prove that when the covariance eigenvalues decay as a power law with index <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the finite-sample bias–variance tradeoff yields:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha\;=\;\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha\;=\;\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularity of the target function and <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies redundancy. Crucially, this result explains why <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is not universal: more redundant representations (flatter spectra) slow down learning, while reducing redundancy (steeper spectra) accelerates scaling. We further establish universality: the redundancy law is invariant under bounded representation transforms, stable under multi-modal mixtures, and robust in finite-width approximations. Extending beyond kernel methods, we demonstrate that the same principle governs scaling in linearized Transformers (NTK regime) and persists under feature learning with kernel drift.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contributions.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive a closed-form scaling exponent <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, linking generalization rates directly to spectral redundancy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove universality: the law holds under representation changes, multi-modal mixtures, and finite-width approximations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the framework to deep architectures, including linearized and feature-learning regimes of Transformers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the unified theoretical foundation explaining empirical scaling laws as consequences of redundancy, elevating them from empirical curves to a principled <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite-sample redundancy law</em>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This redundancy perspective offers both theory and practice: it not only resolves the mystery of scaling laws’ mathematical origin but also prescribes concrete strategies for improving scaling efficiency by reducing redundancy in data representations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20721v1/alpha_vs_redundancy.png" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/338;" width="476" height="338" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Scaling exponent <math id="S1.F1.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> decreases monotonically with redundancy index <math id="S1.F1.sf1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Different curves correspond to source smoothness <math id="S1.F1.sf1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20721v1/scaling_laws_nature.png" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/338;" width="476" height="338" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Log–log learning curves of excess risk vs. sample size under polynomial spectral tails. Different redundancy levels <math id="S1.F1.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> yield different slopes <math id="S1.F1.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Scaling laws as redundancy laws.
(a) Redundancy–exponent relationship.
(b) Learning curves with different redundancy levels.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that scaling laws are not mysterious universal accidents but arise
directly from the bias–variance tradeoff when the data/operator spectrum has
a polynomial (redundant) tail. Concretely, the exponent of the
power-law learning curve is determined by the redundancy index of the spectrum.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For nonnegative functions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> depending on a small/large parameter,
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\lesssim b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\lesssim b</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq C\,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq C\,b</annotation></semantics></math> for an absolute constant <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> independent of
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,\lambda,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,\lambda,m</annotation></semantics></math> etc.; <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\asymp b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≍</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\asymp b</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\lesssim b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\lesssim b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lesssim a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lesssim a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be the covariance (or kernel integral) operator with eigenpairs
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{i},u_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{i},u_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\cdots&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\cdots&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{i}\}_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{i}\}_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> form an orthonormal basis of the RKHS. We measure risk by
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(f)=\mathbb{E}[(f(x)-y)^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(f)=\mathbb{E}[(f(x)-y)^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and excess risk
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(f)=L(f)-L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(f)=L(f)-L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Bayes risk.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumption R (Spectral redundancy / tail).</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c_{-}\leq c_{+}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c_{-}\leq c_{+}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for all <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{-}\,i^{-1/\beta}\;\leq\;\lambda_{i}\;\leq\;c_{+}\,i^{-1/\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{-}\,i^{-1/\beta}\;\leq\;\lambda_{i}\;\leq\;c_{+}\,i^{-1/\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Define the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">redundancy index</em> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mathrm{red}}:=1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mathrm{red}}:=1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumption S (Source condition / target smoothness).</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exist <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\langle g,u_{i}\rangle^{2}\leq C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\langle g,u_{i}\rangle^{2}\leq C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f^{*},u_{i}\rangle\;=\;\lambda_{i}^{\,s}\,\langle g,u_{i}\rangle\quad\forall i\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f^{*},u_{i}\rangle\;=\;\lambda_{i}^{\,s}\,\langle g,u_{i}\rangle\quad\forall i\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimator.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use kernel ridge regression with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> samples and regularization <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective dimension</em>
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda):=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\frac{\lambda_{i}}{\lambda_{i}+\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda):=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\frac{\lambda_{i}}{\lambda_{i}+\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under Assumptions R–S, the canonical bias–variance bound (e.g., Caponnetto–De Vito 2007) reads</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda,n})\;\lesssim\;\underbrace{\lambda^{2s}}_{\text{bias}}\;+\;\underbrace{\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\,N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)}_{\text{variance}}\;\asymp\;\lambda^{2s}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\,\lambda^{-1/\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≲</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>bias</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>variance</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda,n})\;\lesssim\;\underbrace{\lambda^{2s}}_{\text{bias}}\;+\;\underbrace{\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\,N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)}_{\text{variance}}\;\asymp\;\lambda^{2s}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\,\lambda^{-1/\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Uses Appendix A1: Lemma <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Counting function and effective dimension ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, A2: Lemma <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Bias bound from the source condition ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> for bias, A3: Lemma <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Variance bound via effective dimension ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> for variance.)</em></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Scaling law with redundancy-controlled exponent)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(\lambda):=A\,\lambda^{2s}+B\,(\sigma^{2}/n)\lambda^{-1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\lambda):=A\,\lambda^{2s}+B\,(\sigma^{2}/n)\lambda^{-1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> for
constants <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The unique minimizer satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\star}\;=\;\Big(\frac{B}{A}\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\frac{1}{2s}\frac{\beta}{1}\Big)^{\!\frac{1}{2s+1/\beta}}\;\asymp\;n^{-\frac{1}{2s+1/\beta}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\star}\;=\;\Big(\frac{B}{A}\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\frac{1}{2s}\frac{\beta}{1}\Big)^{\!\frac{1}{2s+1/\beta}}\;\asymp\;n^{-\frac{1}{2s+1/\beta}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\quad\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha\;=\;\frac{2s}{\,2s+\frac{1}{\beta}\,}\;=\;\frac{2s}{\,2s+\rho_{\mathrm{red}}\,}.\quad}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>red</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\quad\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha\;=\;\frac{2s}{\,2s+\frac{1}{\beta}\,}\;=\;\frac{2s}{\,2s+\rho_{\mathrm{red}}\,}.\quad}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph">Proof sketch.</em><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Optimize (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Estimator. ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) using <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>;
strict convexity follows since <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Full details are in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.
<em id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(Explicitly: Appendix A1 for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Appendix A4: <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> for the calculus and convexity.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretation.</span> The exponent <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is not universal but explicitly
controlled by redundancy. Steeper spectra (larger <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) yield larger <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,
meaning faster returns to scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Redundancy controls the slope)<span id="Thmcorollary1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(\beta,s)=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(\beta,s)=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is strictly increasing in <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>
(equivalently, strictly decreasing in <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>).
As <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (vanishing redundancy), <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> (fast <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> rate);
as <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\downarrow 1^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\downarrow 1^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> (heavy redundancy), <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\frac{2s}{2s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\frac{2s}{2s+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(Follows immediately from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Scaling law with redundancy-controlled exponent). ‣ Estimator. ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; see Appendix A4 for the derivative check.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Practical reading)<span id="Thmremark1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The log–log learning curve is a straight line with slope <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
Its slope is <em id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">not universal</em> but dictated by redundancy.
Reducing redundancy (steeper eigenvalue decay, i.e. larger <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>)
increases <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, improving “returns to scale”.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next show that this redundancy mechanism is <em id="S2.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">universal</em>, persisting
under representation changes, mixtures of domains, and purification.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Representation invariance)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{H}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a boundedly-invertible linear map with
frame bounds <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;m\leq\|Av\|/\|v\|\leq M&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;m\leq\|Av\|/\|v\|\leq M&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the encoded features
<math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto A\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto A\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> induce a covariance <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=AT_{K}A^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=AT_{K}A^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> that shares the
same tail index <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N^{\prime}_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the optimal rate exponent is unchanged:
<math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Proof sketch.</em> Spectral tails are preserved up to constants by boundedly-invertible transforms. See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.
<em id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(This is Appendix A5: <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mixtures of domains)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\sum_{k=1}^{K}w_{k}T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\sum_{k=1}^{K}w_{k}T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex combination of positive compact operators.
If <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has tail index <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\min_{k}\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\min_{k}\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so the achievable exponent is <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Proof sketch.</em> The heaviest (flattest) tail dominates. See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>.
<em id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(This is Appendix A6: <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Redundancy reduction improves <math id="Thmproposition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If a transformation produces <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with a steeper tail <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}\geq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}\geq\beta</annotation></semantics></math>,
then the exponent improves:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}\;=\;\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta^{\prime}}\;\geq\;\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}\;=\;\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta^{\prime}}\;\geq\;\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph">Proof sketch.</em><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Effective dimension shrinks with steeper spectra. See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS7" title="A.7 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>.
<em id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(This is Appendix A7: <a href="#A1.SS7" title="A.7 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Architectural proxies)<span id="Thmremark2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In wide-network or random-features regimes that approximate kernels,
Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Representation invariance). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Mixtures of domains). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> apply provided the induced kernel
retains its spectral tail up to constants. Thus the power-law exponent is a
property of the <em id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">data–representation pair</em>, not the architecture.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Universality takeaway.</h5>

<div id="S2.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Representation changes (boundedly-invertible) preserve <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Appendix A5.)</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixtures are governed by the heaviest tail.
<em id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Appendix A6.)</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spectral purification (redundancy reduction) monotonically increases <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Appendix A7.)</em></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hence scaling laws and their slopes are universal consequences of spectral redundancy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Non-i.i.d. data: mixing and effective sample size</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a strictly stationary sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{t},y_{t})\}_{t=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{t},y_{t})\}_{t=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with strong mixing.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta(k)\}_{k\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta(k)\}_{k\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing coefficients. Assume</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\beta(k)^{\frac{\gamma}{2+\gamma}}\;&lt;\;\infty\quad\text{for some }\gamma&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for some </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\beta(k)^{\frac{\gamma}{2+\gamma}}\;&lt;\;\infty\quad\text{for some }\gamma&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and finite <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2+\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2+\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>-moments for noise and features.
Define the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective sample size</em></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}\;:=\;\frac{n}{\,1+2\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\big(1-\tfrac{k}{n}\big)\,\rho(k)\,},\qquad\rho(k)\;\asymp\;\beta(k)^{\frac{\gamma}{2+\gamma}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}\;:=\;\frac{n}{\,1+2\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\big(1-\tfrac{k}{n}\big)\,\rho(k)\,},\qquad\rho(k)\;\asymp\;\beta(k)^{\frac{\gamma}{2+\gamma}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Scaling law with mixing)<span id="Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With Assumptions R and S, and <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing data of effective size <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the optimal regularization obeys
<math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda^{\star}\asymp n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{-1/(2s+1/\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≍</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>eff</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\star}\asymp n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{-1/(2s+1/\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph">Proof sketch.</em><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Variance bounds under mixing inflate <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>. See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS9" title="A.9 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>.
<em id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(Uses Appendix A8: <a href="#A1.SS8" title="A.8 Variance bound under mixing ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a> for variance inflation, and Appendix A9: <a href="#A1.SS9" title="A.9 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a> for the optimization with <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Dependence effect)<span id="Thmremark3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Short-range dependence (<math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}\rho(k)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}\rho(k)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>) yields <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}\asymp n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>≍</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}\asymp n</annotation></semantics></math>,
so exponent matches i.i.d. case. Long-range dependence slows the clock but
leaves <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged.
<em id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(See Appendix A8–A9.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Finite-width random features: spectral approximation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be the true kernel and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> its <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> -feature random approximation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumption (Spectral approximation).</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exist <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0},C_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0},C_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> s.t., w.h.p.,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|T_{m}-T_{K}\|_{\mathrm{op}}\;\leq\;C_{0}\,m^{-r},\qquad\big|\mathrm{Tr}((T_{m}+\lambda I)^{-1}T_{m})-N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\big|\;\leq\;C_{1}\,m^{-r}\,\lambda^{-1/\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi>op</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>Tr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.169em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|T_{m}-T_{K}\|_{\mathrm{op}}\;\leq\;C_{0}\,m^{-r},\qquad\big|\mathrm{Tr}((T_{m}+\lambda I)^{-1}T_{m})-N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\lambda)\big|\;\leq\;C_{1}\,m^{-r}\,\lambda^{-1/\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Scaling with finite width)<span id="Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\gtrsim n^{\,r/(2s+1/\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\gtrsim n^{\,r/(2s+1/\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda^{\star}\asymp n^{-1/(2s+1/\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≍</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\star}\asymp n^{-1/(2s+1/\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n}^{(m)})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n}^{(m)})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph">Proof sketch.</em><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> RF approximation adds a third error term; polynomial width growth suffices. See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS12" title="A.12 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a>.
<em id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(This relies on Appendix A10: <a href="#A1.SS10" title="A.10 Tail index preservation under small perturbations ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.10</span></a> for tail stability of <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, Appendix A11: <a href="#A1.SS11" title="A.11 Bias–variance with RF width ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.11</span></a> for the three-term decomposition, Appendix A12: <a href="#A1.SS12" title="A.12 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a> for the optimization and the <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-scaling.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Practical prescription)<span id="Thmremark4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Width <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> need only grow polynomially with <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (degree <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/(2s+1/\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/(2s+1/\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>).
Finite width changes constants, not the exponent <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(See Appendix A10–A12.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Transformers: from kernels to deep nonlinearity</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transformers dominate modern AI. We now show that their scaling exponents
are governed by the same redundancy principle, both in linearized NTK regimes
and in feature-learning regimes with kernel drift.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Linearized Transformers: NTK / random-features regime</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a Transformer with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> residual blocks, multi-head self-attention
(MHSA) and MLP sub-layers, LayerNorm, and token embeddings. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote initialization and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the parameters at step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of SGD.
We write the <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linearized predictor</em> around <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta}(x)\approx f_{\Theta_{0}}(x)+\nabla_{\Theta}f_{\Theta_{0}}(x)\cdot(\Theta-\Theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta}(x)\approx f_{\Theta_{0}}(x)+\nabla_{\Theta}f_{\Theta_{0}}(x)\cdot(\Theta-\Theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
which induces a Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumption T1 (Transformer NTK tail).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The NTK operator <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the sequence distribution
has eigenvalues <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{i}(T_{\mathrm{tr}})\asymp i^{-1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}(T_{\mathrm{tr}})\asymp i^{-1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for some <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> (polynomial tail), for all <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, LayerNorm and
residual maps are bi-Lipschitz on the data manifold with constants <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;m\leq M&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;m\leq M&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and MHSA with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads
induces a kernel <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{att}}=\sum_{h=1}^{H}T_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>att</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{att}}=\sum_{h=1}^{H}T_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> whose tail index
is <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{att}}=\min_{h=1}^{H}\beta_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>att</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{att}}=\min_{h=1}^{H}\beta_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (heaviest-tail dominates).
The MLP kernel <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math> has tail index <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the block-aggregated kernel obeys
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{tr}}\simeq\sum_{\ell=1}^{L}(T_{\mathrm{att}}^{(\ell)}+T_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{(\ell)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>att</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{tr}}\simeq\sum_{\ell=1}^{L}(T_{\mathrm{att}}^{(\ell)}+T_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{(\ell)})</annotation></semantics></math>
up to boundedly-invertible representation reparametrizations (in the sense of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Representation invariance). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumption T2 (Implicit regularization <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> ridge).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early-stopped SGD with step size <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> steps on the linearized
model is equivalent, in prediction space, to ridge-regularized interpolation
with an <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective</em> <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\asymp(\eta t)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≍</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\asymp(\eta t)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (standard gradient-flow
or discrete-time equivalence, holding in the infinite-width limit or under suitable overparameterization).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer scaling in the linearized regime)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under T1–T2 and the source condition of order <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> in the NTK RKHS,
the excess risk satisfies the bias–variance bound</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda,n})\;\lesssim\;\lambda^{2s}\;+\;\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\,N_{\mathrm{eff}}^{(\mathrm{tr})}(\lambda),\quad N_{\mathrm{eff}}^{(\mathrm{tr})}(\lambda)=\mathrm{Tr}\big((T_{\mathrm{tr}}+\lambda I)^{-1}T_{\mathrm{tr}}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≲</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tr</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tr</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda,n})\;\lesssim\;\lambda^{2s}\;+\;\frac{\sigma^{2}}{n}\,N_{\mathrm{eff}}^{(\mathrm{tr})}(\lambda),\quad N_{\mathrm{eff}}^{(\mathrm{tr})}(\lambda)=\mathrm{Tr}\big((T_{\mathrm{tr}}+\lambda I)^{-1}T_{\mathrm{tr}}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{i}(T_{\mathrm{tr}})\asymp i^{-1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}(T_{\mathrm{tr}})\asymp i^{-1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N_{\mathrm{eff}}^{(\mathrm{tr})}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>eff</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>tr</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{eff}}^{(\mathrm{tr})}(\lambda)\asymp\lambda^{-1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and with the optimal <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda^{\star}\asymp n^{-1/(2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≍</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\star}\asymp n^{-1/(2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}})}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}},\qquad\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}\;=\;\frac{2s}{\,2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}\,}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}},\qquad\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}\;=\;\frac{2s}{\,2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}\,}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph">Proof.</em><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS13" title="A.13 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.13</span></a>.
<em id="Thmtheorem6.p1.3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(This is Appendix A13: <a href="#A1.SS13" title="A.13 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.13</span></a>, which relies on Appendix A5–A6 for invariance/mixture transfer and T2 for SGD<math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>ridge.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5</span></span><span id="Thmremark5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(What determines <math id="Thmremark5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math>?)<span id="Thmremark5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tail is jointly shaped by token statistics (Zipfian long tail),
context structure (non-i.i.d. dependencies), attention locality, and head diversity.
Our mixture and invariance results imply <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimum
tail index among constituent attention and MLP kernels after bounded reparametrizations.
<em id="Thmremark5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(Appeals to Appendix A5–A6.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Beyond linearization: feature learning with adiabatic kernel drift</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Real Transformers are not purely lazy; the effective kernel evolves with training.
We formalize a regime where the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tail index</em> remains stable while constants
drift.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumption T3 (Adiabatic tail stability).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the instantaneous effective kernel along SGD at step <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. There exist
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{min}}\!\leq\!\beta_{\mathrm{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{min}}\!\leq\!\beta_{\mathrm{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\!&gt;\!0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\!&gt;\!0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq t\!\leq\!T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq t\!\leq\!T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C^{-1}\,i^{-1/\beta_{\max}}\;\leq\;\lambda_{i}(T_{t})\;\leq\;C\,i^{-1/\beta_{\min}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-1}\,i^{-1/\beta_{\max}}\;\leq\;\lambda_{i}(T_{t})\;\leq\;C\,i^{-1/\beta_{\min}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the per-epoch drift of the resolvent is controlled:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|(T_{t+1}+\lambda I)^{-1}-(T_{t}+\lambda I)^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq\Delta_{t}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|(T_{t+1}+\lambda I)^{-1}-(T_{t}+\lambda I)^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq\Delta_{t}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\Delta_{t}(\lambda^{\star})=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\Delta_{t}(\lambda^{\star})=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen optimally as in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Scaling law with redundancy-controlled exponent). ‣ Estimator. ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer scaling with kernel drift)<span id="Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under T3 and the source condition, the bias–variance analysis with the
time-averaged kernel <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{T}=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{T}=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha_{\mathrm{drift}}},\qquad\alpha_{\mathrm{drift}}\in\Big[\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\max}},\;\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\min}}\Big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≍</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>drift</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>drift</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\,\mathcal{E}(f_{\lambda^{\star},n})\;\asymp\;n^{-\alpha_{\mathrm{drift}}},\qquad\alpha_{\mathrm{drift}}\in\Big[\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\max}},\;\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\min}}\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, if the tail index is stable (<math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\min}=\beta_{\max}=\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\min}=\beta_{\max}=\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math>),
then <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{drift}}=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>drift</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{drift}}=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, identical to the linearized case.
<em id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Proof.</em> See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS14" title="A.14 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.14</span></a>.
<em id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(This is Appendix A14: <a href="#A1.SS14" title="A.14 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.14</span></a>.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6</span></span><span id="Thmremark6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(When feature learning helps)<span id="Thmremark6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If training steepens the tail (redundancy reduction, i.e. <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> increases),
then by Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="proposition 1 (Redundancy reduction improves 𝛼). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> the exponent <em id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">improves</em> monotonically.
Thus feature learning that reduces redundancy is predicted to yield <em id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">larger</em>
scaling exponents (faster returns).
<em id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(Uses Appendix A7.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>SGD, non-i.i.d. sequences, and context length</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For language, samples are not i.i.d. tokens; they are sequences with dependencies.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumption T4 (Sequence mixing and effective sample).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over sequences of length <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose the per-sequence gradients form a
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing process across the dataset with mixing profile <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(k)</annotation></semantics></math> as in
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Scaling law with mixing). ‣ 2.1 Non-i.i.d. data: mixing and effective sample size ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, satisfying <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}\rho(k)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}\rho(k)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> or allowing long-range dependence. Then the variance term scales with an effective
sample size <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the exponent is unchanged:
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Scaling with context)<span id="Thmcorollary2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fixing <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> changes constants but not the exponent. If <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> grows with <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> so that
the effective kernel adds heads/bands with tails no heavier than existing ones (i.e., new tail indices <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}\geq\min\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}\geq\min\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math>),
the tail index remains <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the exponent is preserved.
<em id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Proof.</em> See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS15" title="A.15 Proof of Corollary (Scaling with context) ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.15</span></a>.
<em id="Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(This is Appendix A15: <a href="#A1.SS15" title="A.15 Proof of Corollary (Scaling with context) ‣ Appendix A Proofs and Detailed Derivations ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.15</span></a>, and it relies on Appendix A6 for mixture domination, A8–A9 for mixing-based variance control.)</em></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Summary and prescriptions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exponent driver.</span> Transformer scaling exponents are governed by the
<em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tail index</em> <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the effective kernel (NTK or its
time-average). Universality results (representation invariance, mixtures) carry
over to multi-head, residual stacks, and LayerNorm. <em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Appendix A5–A6, A13–A15.)</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SGD <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> ridge.</span> Early stopping or small-step SGD
acts as ridge with <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\asymp(\eta t)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≍</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\asymp(\eta t)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; optimizing <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> yields
<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{tr}}=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Appendix A4 for the calculus; A13 for the NTK instantiation.)</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature learning.</span> If training <em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">steepens</em> the tail
(reduces redundancy), the exponent <em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">increases</em> monotonically.
<em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Appendix A7; also A14 in the drift setting.)</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-i.i.d.</span> Dependencies slow the clock via <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> but
leave the exponent unchanged. <em id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Appendix A8–A9; context extension in A15.)</em></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Representation invariance of scaling laws</h4>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.20721v1/invariance_learning_curves_nature_dpi600.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/207;" width="310" height="207" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Invariance of scaling laws under representation transforms.</span>
Learning curves of excess risk versus sample size <math id="S4.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> under boundedly invertible linear transforms of the feature representation.
The baseline identity representation (orange) and three randomly generated transforms (blue/green/yellow) exhibit nearly identical power-law scaling behavior, confirming Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Representation invariance). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> that the scaling exponent <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant to such transformations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine the invariance property predicted by our theory, we conducted an experiment in which the feature representation was modified by applying boundedly invertible linear transforms. Each transform was constructed by combining a random orthogonal rotation with a diagonal rescaling drawn from a fixed range. Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Representation invariance of scaling laws ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the resulting learning curves of excess risk as a function of sample size <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on a log–log scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results show that the transformed representations yield almost identical learning curves to the original identity representation, with slopes that collapse precisely onto the same power-law trend. This invariance confirms the theoretical prediction of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Representation invariance). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which states that the tail index <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> of the covariance spectrum—and consequently the scaling exponent <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>—is preserved under boundedly invertible reparameterizations of the feature space. In practical terms, this demonstrates that scaling behavior is an intrinsic property of the data distribution’s redundancy structure, rather than an artifact of the chosen coordinate system or representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Effect of mixing on effective sample size</h4>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20721v1/mixing_experiment_vs_n_theory_neff.png" id="S4.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span><span id="S4.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Error vs. sample size <math id="S4.F3.sf1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Excess risk decreases with <math id="S4.F3.sf1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> under different AR(1) dependence strengths <math id="S4.F3.sf1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
Curves for <math id="S4.F3.sf1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.2,0.5,0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.2,0.5,0.8</annotation></semantics></math> are shifted vertically, but exhibit similar slopes.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20721v1/mixing_experiment_vs_neff_theory.png" id="S4.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span><span id="S4.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Collapse vs. effective sample size <math id="S4.F3.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
When plotted against <math id="S4.F3.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}=n\frac{1-\rho}{1+\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}=n\frac{1-\rho}{1+\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>,
all curves collapse onto a common power law, confirming Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Scaling law with mixing). ‣ 2.1 Non-i.i.d. data: mixing and effective sample size ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mixing experiment.</span>
(a) Under temporal dependence modeled by AR(1) with coefficient <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>,
scaling with the raw sample size <math id="S4.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> shows apparent differences across <math id="S4.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
(b) Re-parameterizing in terms of effective sample size <math id="S4.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> removes these discrepancies,
demonstrating that mixing slows the “clock” but does not alter the scaling exponent <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next investigated the impact of temporal dependence on scaling behavior.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ Effect of mixing on effective sample size ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> shows learning curves when data are generated from an AR(1) process with varying autocorrelation coefficient <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
While higher dependence (<math id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>) yields larger errors at a given <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
all curves display similar slopes on the log–log scale, consistent with our theoretical prediction that the exponent <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is unaffected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize this, we reparameterized the <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis in terms of the effective sample size <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}=n(1-\rho)/(1+\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}=n(1-\rho)/(1+\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ Effect of mixing on effective sample size ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>, the three curves collapse onto a single scaling law,
validating Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Scaling law with mixing). ‣ 2.1 Non-i.i.d. data: mixing and effective sample size ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
This confirms that temporal dependence reduces the rate at which effective samples accumulate,
but does not change the redundancy-controlled scaling exponent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Mixture of domains: heavy-tail dominance</h4>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.20721v1/mixture_experiment_nature_dpi600.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/207;" width="310" height="207" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mixture experiment: heavy-tail dominates.</span>
Learning curve of excess risk for a mixture of two domains with spectral tails <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=1.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=1.3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=2.5</annotation></semantics></math>, combined with weights <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}=0.6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}=0.4</annotation></semantics></math>.
The observed scaling is governed by the heavier tail (smaller <math id="S4.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Mixtures of domains). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which predicts <math id="S4.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\min\{\beta_{1},\beta_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\min\{\beta_{1},\beta_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next investigated the behavior of scaling laws under multi-domain mixtures.
In this setting, the overall covariance operator is formed as a convex combination of two component operators with distinct spectral decays, here chosen with tail indices <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=1.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=1.3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=2.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Mixture of domains: heavy-tail dominance ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the resulting learning curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results reveal that the scaling exponent is dictated not by the average or the lighter tail, but by the heaviest-tailed component.
Specifically, the observed power-law decay aligns with the theoretical prediction <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\min\{\beta_{1},\beta_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\min\{\beta_{1},\beta_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, as formalized in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Mixtures of domains). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
This finding highlights a dominance principle: in heterogeneous or multi-modal data distributions, the slowest spectral decay controls the overall scaling law.
In practice, this implies that even if part of the data admits fast convergence, the presence of a heavy-tailed component can bottleneck learning efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Spectral tails and redundancy index</h4>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.20721v1/spectrum_comparison.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:310/258;" width="310" height="258" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spectra comparison across tail indices <math id="S4.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Eigenvalue decay of covariance operators with polynomial spectral tails <math id="S4.F5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{i}\propto i^{-1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\propto i^{-1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\{1.2,1.5,2.0,5.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\{1.2,1.5,2.0,5.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Smaller <math id="S4.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values correspond to flatter spectra (heavier tails), indicating higher redundancy,
while larger <math id="S4.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> yield faster decay and thus lower redundancy.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the role of spectral decay in controlling redundancy,
Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Spectral tails and redundancy index ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> plots eigenvalue spectra with different polynomial tail indices <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The decay rate directly determines the effective redundancy index <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
For small <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.2</annotation></semantics></math>), the spectrum decays slowly, implying that a large number of eigen-directions contribute significantly to variance.
This corresponds to high redundancy and results in slower learning curves.
Conversely, for large <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=5.0</annotation></semantics></math>), eigenvalues decay sharply, variance concentrates in the leading directions, and redundancy is reduced, leading to faster scaling exponents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This visualization highlights the central principle of our theory:
the spectral tail index <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> acts as the fundamental parameter that governs redundancy,
and thereby determines the slope of the scaling law <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Finite-width random features</h4>

<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.20721v1/theorylambda.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/218;" width="310" height="218" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random Fourier Features with theory <math id="S4.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Learning curves of excess risk versus sample size <math id="S4.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for different random feature widths <math id="S4.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{50,100,200,500,1000,2000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>50,100,200,500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{50,100,200,500,1000,2000\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
All curves follow the predicted power-law trend at small <math id="S4.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, but finite-width approximation introduces additional variance and distortion in the intermediate regime.
Larger <math id="S4.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> values reduce this distortion and converge closer to the kernel scaling law, in agreement with Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5 (Scaling with finite width). ‣ Assumption (Spectral approximation). ‣ 2.2 Finite-width random features: spectral approximation ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next examined the finite-width setting by replacing the kernel with random Fourier features (RFF) of varying width <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Finite-width random features ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the learning curves under the theoretical regularization schedule <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\star}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\star}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
At small sample sizes, all curves exhibit the expected power-law decay, but clear deviations appear in the intermediate regime.
These “humps” reflect the finite-width approximation error, which acts as an additional source of bias not accounted for by the infinite-width analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As predicted by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5 (Scaling with finite width). ‣ Assumption (Spectral approximation). ‣ 2.2 Finite-width random features: spectral approximation ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, increasing the feature width <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> mitigates this effect:
for <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1000</annotation></semantics></math>, the curves align more closely with the kernel scaling law, while smaller widths (e.g., <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=50,100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50,100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=50,100</annotation></semantics></math>) remain dominated by approximation noise.
This confirms that finite-width models inherit the same scaling exponent <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, but only once <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> grows sufficiently fast with <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, width-induced variance distorts the learning curve.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Synthetic verification of redundancy laws</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Synthetic verification of redundancy laws ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the synthetic experiments provide a direct validation of the redundancy-law predictions.
Panel (a) demonstrates that in a representative setting (<math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>), the fitted slope of the learning curve closely matches the theoretical exponent, confirming that the excess risk follows a power-law decay.
Panel (b) highlights the role of source smoothness <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>: smoother targets yield faster convergence, exactly as predicted by the formula <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, Panel (c) illustrates the impact of spectral tails: heavier-tailed spectra (smaller <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) increase redundancy, resulting in shallower curves, while larger <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> steepens the slope by reducing redundancy.
Together, these results establish that both <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> act as fundamental levers governing the scaling exponent <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and validate the theoretical predictions under controlled Gaussian settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20721v1/synthetic_fitcheck_beta2_s05.png" id="S4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span><span id="S4.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fit check.</span> Example case with <math id="S4.F7.sf1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F7.sf1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
The fitted slope <math id="S4.F7.sf1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is close to the predicted <math id="S4.F7.sf1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}=0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}=0.67</annotation></semantics></math>,
confirming power-law behavior.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20721v1/synthetic_learning_curves_beta2.png" id="S4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span><span id="S4.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Varying source smoothness <math id="S4.F7.sf2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
For fixed <math id="S4.F7.sf2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, larger <math id="S4.F7.sf2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> accelerates error decay,
consistent with <math id="S4.F7.sf2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\tfrac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.20721v1/synthetic_learning_curves_s05.png" id="S4.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span><span id="S4.F7.sf3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Varying spectral tail <math id="S4.F7.sf3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
For fixed <math id="S4.F7.sf3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing <math id="S4.F7.sf3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> steepens the curve,
reflecting the benefit of reduced redundancy.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic validation of scaling law predictions.</span>
Across controlled Gaussian data, learning curves follow the predicted power-law decay.
Panel (a) shows a representative fit check; panel (b) verifies the effect of source smoothness <math id="S4.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>;
and panel (c) confirms that the heavy-tailed spectrum with small <math id="S4.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> slows convergence.
Together, these experiments validate Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Scaling law with redundancy-controlled exponent). ‣ Estimator. ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and demonstrate that both <math id="S4.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> jointly control the scaling exponent <math id="S4.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these experiments confirm that redundancy laws provide a unifying account of scaling behavior across transformations, dependencies, mixtures, and approximations. This suggests that empirical scaling laws observed in practice are not artifacts of models or datasets, but reflect a deeper universality rooted in spectral redundancy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Foundational Insight: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work establishes a foundational theoretical framework for understanding scaling laws in machine learning, demonstrating that these empirical phenomena are fundamentally <em id="S5.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">redundancy laws</em>. By deriving a closed-form expression for the scaling exponent <math id="S5.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> in the kernel regression setting, we provide the first rigorous mathematical explanation for why excess risk decays as a power law with sample size <math id="S5.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and how <math id="S5.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is explicitly controlled by the spectral redundancy index <math id="S5.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>red</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\mathrm{red}}=1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and the source smoothness <math id="S5.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. This result not only demystifies the origin of power-law behavior but also reveals that scaling exponents are not universal constants, as often assumed in empirical studies, but rather tunable parameters tied to the intrinsic redundancy of the data representation. For instance, in regimes with heavy redundancy (small <math id="S5.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>), learning curves are shallower, while reducing redundancy (larger <math id="S5.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) accelerates improvement, aligning with intuitive notions from complex systems theory where redundant structures slow down information extraction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Geometrically, <math id="S5.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> controls how fast the covariance spectrum concentrates on low-index directions: larger <math id="S5.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> means more “compressible” signal and fewer effective degrees of freedom. Analytically,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\alpha}{\partial\beta}=\frac{2s}{(2s+1/\beta)^{2}}\,\frac{1}{\beta^{2}}\;&gt;\;0,\qquad\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\alpha=1,\quad\lim_{\beta\downarrow 1^{+}}\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\alpha}{\partial\beta}=\frac{2s}{(2s+1/\beta)^{2}}\,\frac{1}{\beta^{2}}\;&gt;\;0,\qquad\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\alpha=1,\quad\lim_{\beta\downarrow 1^{+}}\alpha=\frac{2s}{2s+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which formalizes the monotone benefit of redundancy reduction and quantifies the asymptotic walls in both benign and heavy-redundancy regimes. Operationally, the theory prescribes two orthogonal levers: (i) increase <math id="S5.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> via better target-regularity alignment (representation/label smoothing), and (ii) increase <math id="S5.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> via redundancy reduction (spectral purification).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Universality Across Representations and Domains</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The universality results further strengthen the framework’s robustness. Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Representation invariance). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows invariance under boundedly invertible transformations, ensuring the law holds across equivalent data representations. Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Mixtures of domains). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> demonstrates that multi-domain mixtures are dominated by the heaviest tail, explaining why scaling laws persist in multi-modal settings like vision-language models. Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="proposition 1 (Redundancy reduction improves 𝛼). ‣ Universality: representation invariance and multi-domain mixtures ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> offers a practical pathway for optimization: spectral purification techniques, such as advanced feature engineering or architecture modifications, can monotonically improve <math id="S5.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, providing actionable insights for model designers. Extensions to non-i.i.d. data (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Scaling law with mixing). ‣ 2.1 Non-i.i.d. data: mixing and effective sample size ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and finite-width random features (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5 (Scaling with finite width). ‣ Assumption (Spectral approximation). ‣ 2.2 Finite-width random features: spectral approximation ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) bridge the gap to realistic scenarios, showing that dependencies and finite overparameterization merely adjust constants (via <math id="S5.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> or polynomial width growth) without altering the exponent. Finally, the application to Transformers (Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (Transformer scaling in the linearized regime). ‣ Assumption T2 (Implicit regularization ≈ ridge). ‣ 3.1 Linearized Transformers: NTK / random-features regime ‣ 3 Transformers: from kernels to deep nonlinearity ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7 (Transformer scaling with kernel drift). ‣ Assumption T3 (Adiabatic tail stability). ‣ 3.2 Beyond linearization: feature learning with adiabatic kernel drift ‣ 3 Transformers: from kernels to deep nonlinearity ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) unifies the theory with state-of-the-art architectures, confirming that both linearized NTK regimes and feature-learning dynamics with adiabatic kernel drift inherit the redundancy-controlled scaling, thus explaining the consistent power-law observations across NLP, CV, and beyond.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Practical Implications</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practical terms, our framework elevates scaling laws from mere empirical fits to predictive tools. For example, estimating <math id="S5.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> from data spectra could forecast optimal scaling strategies, guiding resource allocation in large-scale training. Moreover, the emphasis on redundancy reduction suggests novel directions for improving efficiency, such as data deduplication, adaptive representations, or architectures that explicitly minimize spectral redundancy during pre-training. By closing the gap between empirical observations and mathematical principles, this work provides a unifying lens for interpreting scaling behaviors in complex systems, potentially influencing fields beyond machine learning, such as statistical physics or information theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Limitations</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advances, our analysis has several limitations that merit careful consideration. First, the core results rely on specific assumptions, such as the polynomial spectral tail (Assumption <a href="#S2.E1" title="In Assumption R (Spectral redundancy / tail). ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and source condition (Assumption <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Assumption S (Source condition / target smoothness). ‣ 2 Main Result: Scaling Laws as Redundancy Laws ‣ Scaling Laws are Redundancy Laws" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which, while empirically motivated (e.g., Zipfian distributions in NLP), may not hold universally across all data distributions. For instance, non-polynomial tails (e.g., exponential or logarithmic decay) could lead to different scaling forms, and relaxing these to more general regular variation classes might require additional technical tools. Second, the non-i.i.d. extension (Section on mixing) assumes <math id="S5.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing with summable coefficients, which covers short- and moderate-range dependencies but may not fully capture strong long-range correlations common in natural data, such as fractal processes or heavy-tailed dependencies in time series. In such cases, the effective sample size <math id="S5.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> could decay sublinearly, potentially altering the effective scaling regime in ways not yet quantified. Third, the finite-width and Transformer extensions introduce approximations: the spectral approximation assumption for random features assumes controlled operator-norm perturbations, which holds in infinite-width limits but may degrade for finite widths below the required polynomial growth <math id="S5.SSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\gtrsim n^{r/(2s+1/\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\gtrsim n^{r/(2s+1/\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, the adiabatic drift assumption in feature learning (Assumption T3) posits bounded resolvent changes, but real Transformer training often exhibits more abrupt kernel shifts due to phase transitions or non-convex optimization landscapes, which could invalidate the exponent interval bounds. Fourth, while we bridge to Transformers via NTK and drift models, the analysis remains at a high level, relying on effective kernels without explicit derivations of <math id="S5.SSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> from architectural primitives like attention mechanisms or positional encodings. Finally, the current work is purely theoretical, lacking numerical validations on real datasets, which limits empirical corroboration of predicted <math id="S5.SSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values or redundancy estimates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Future Directions</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These limitations open rich avenues for future research. Empirically, validating the framework on large-scale datasets—such as estimating <math id="S5.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> from covariance spectra in NLP corpora or CV features—could confirm theoretical predictions and quantify redundancy in practice. For instance, spectral analysis of pre-trained embeddings might reveal domain-specific <math id="S5.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values, enabling tailored scaling strategies. Theoretically, extending beyond polynomial tails to general slowly varying functions or incorporating non-stationary distributions could broaden applicability. In the Transformer context, deriving explicit bounds on <math id="S5.SSx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> from attention and MLP layers, perhaps using operator theory on graphs or random matrix approximations, would provide finer-grained insights. Exploring beyond ridge-equivalent regularization, such as full SGD trajectories with momentum or adaptive optimizers, could refine the implicit bias assumptions (Assumption T2). Additionally, integrating redundancy laws with other paradigms, like information bottlenecks or double descent phenomena, might yield hybrid models explaining overparameterized regimes. Finally, practical extensions could focus on redundancy reduction algorithms, such as learned spectral filters or data augmentation techniques, to empirically boost <math id="S5.SSx5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in real models. Overall, while our work lays a solid theoretical foundation, addressing these gaps could transform redundancy laws into a comprehensive toolkit for scaling efficient AI systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
